  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-112468",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{1}{2}) & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}-3(-\\frac{1}{2})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
